{"item": "s05_commented_noise.py", "draw": 2, "score": 48.0, "label": null, "latency_s": 13.655, "exit": 0, "stdout": "```json\n{\"score\": 48}\n```\n\nThe core algorithm is clear and straightforward, but the excessive redundant comments actively harm readability. Each meaningful line is followed by 1\u20132 comments restating what the code already says plainly (e.g., `sorted(values) # sorted`, `n = len(values) # length`). This creates signal-to-noise friction that forces a reader to wade through narration to grasp simple operations. Comments should explain *why*, not echo *what*\u2014the code itself is clear enough that these distract rather than clarify.\n", "stderr": "", "ts": 1791313001.3944547}
{"item": "s05_commented_noise.py", "draw": 1, "score": 52.0, "label": null, "latency_s": 16.058, "exit": 0, "stdout": "```json\n{\"score\": 52}\n```\n\nThe core logic is clean and straightforward, but excessive trivial comments (\"# sort the values\", \"# odd\", \"# middle\") clutter the signal-to-noise ratio. Comments should explain *why*, not restate what the code already says clearly. A stranger with any Python literacy finds these distracting; a stranger without it doesn't benefit because the code is already self-documenting. The function deserves higher marks absent the commentary\u2014around 75. With it, readability drops below neutral.\n", "stderr": "", "ts": 1791313001.8807445}
