{"item": "s08_magic.py", "draw": 4, "score": 19.0, "label": null, "latency_s": 16.062, "exit": 0, "stdout": "```json\n{\"score\": 19}\n```\n\nHostile to reader. No function name (what is `f` supposed to do?), no parameter intent (`x`, `y` could be anything), magic numbers (0.0174533, 3.14159, 6.28319) without explanation, inconsistent representation (why both 0.0174533 and 3.14159/180 for the same conversion?), no docstring. A stranger would have to reverse-engineer that this combines angle values in degrees, converts to radians, and normalizes to [0, 2\u03c0). They cannot understand it in one read.\n", "stderr": "", "ts": 1791313050.4547842}
{"item": "s08_magic.py", "draw": 6, "score": 18.0, "label": null, "latency_s": 14.444, "exit": 0, "stdout": "```json\n{\"score\": 18}\n```\n\n**Why so low:** Function name `f` is non-descriptive. Variable names `x`, `y` give no context. Magic numbers (0.0174533, 3.14159, 180, 6.28319) are unexplained\u2014a reader must recognize these as \u03c0/180 and 2\u03c0 to understand angle conversion. No docstring. A stranger cannot understand intent in one read.\n", "stderr": "", "ts": 1791313050.7590704}
